💡 **SUGGESTION: pin the native-adopted `status` surface — the twin of this foreign-side assert** (registry F1, open since round 1 — the last unpinned headline surface)

The new group drives `bash_job status` through the real tool only for the **foreign** side (`no captured log`, asserted here). The status behavior this PR actually changes — a native-adopted job showing `log: <path>` via `_shellJobStatusLine`'s `entry.isAdopted && entry.logPath.isEmpty` branch (`builtin_tools_bash.dart:1437`) — still has no assertion anywhere: the AC1 test asserts `entry.logPath` and `output`, never `status` (verified fresh on head `7395251d78`: no `log: $logPath` status assert exists in this file). The PR description lists this surface as a headline claim ("bash_job status shows 'log: <path>' for native-adopted instead of 'no captured log'"), so it can still regress silently. Round-3's rework fixed the board card and the docs but not this pin.

This file's own convention pins every branch of the adopted renderers ("every branch is unit-pinned in adopted_shell_jobs_test", `agent_cli_waiting.dart:1224`). Twin assert in the AC1 test (or here):

```dart
final status = await _toolText(bashJobTool(registry), {
  'action': 'status',
  'id': 'sh-9-prev',
});
expect(status, contains('log: $logPath'));
expect(status, isNot(contains('no captured log')));
```

